45 "Expressing the Coordinates of a Vector in a Given Basis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120054 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120054 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120054 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120054 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ad4d6410-a365-5f77-a4b1-3e2d134d5a3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120525 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93dd069a-4710-5057-b8fe-736d3ab4c0dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120695 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120695 #edb/ref 1061 17513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55354f89-d587-5329-9788-b198ebb218a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120696 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120696 #edb/ref 1061 17514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120696 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2734184-6a42-50f6-9a19-566236b06025" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120696 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120697 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120697 #edb/ref 1061 17515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120697 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94aa1ebf-d415-58aa-a0dc-17e8201c1669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120697 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120698 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120698 #edb/ref 1061 17516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120698 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34b75a42-9505-5418-a27f-2b9aade2bc07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120698 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120756 #edb/ref 1061 23574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120756 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9053925c-8744-5312-b890-f5e4a1a8d2e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120756 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120757 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120757 #edb/ref 1061 23612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120757 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79095f38-ffd3-565b-a534-f136c7646422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120757 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121042 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n![](images/05/0593ca6a838b66c4623e8749eb5236d6259b1f9bf36ed0d68b83c49781076341.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n![](images/f3/f355faa0c477775f2a1cbb3522d0743cdbc9649a5ee9c55b579b1fac9ae2a661.svg)\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector ![](images/78/78357fcf51c9cd6b7873f5bc35fcc214cb3790bcec00ee69d0932f15aacd39cb.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121042 #edb/ref 1065 3208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121042 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bb4f07e6-7c2f-5fe2-b96b-1bb37603981a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121042 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121066 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider a set of vectors\n![](images/0d/0d78e1b2d01003885882f19b3856278e5024719f26f33594fd071fbd73273b71.svg)\n\nNotice that the set $\\mathcal{S}$ is linearly independent since the vector equation $x_1\\mathbf{e}_1+x_2\\mathbf{e}_2+x_3\\mathbf{e}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ leads to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution. \n\nAlso, notice that any vector in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{e}_1,$ $\\mathbf{e}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{e}_3.$ For instance,\n![](images/ef/ef28dd8cbad6d256b9fa82c487263af3327ce630839cfcd1c7433417d0e0ae73.svg)\n\nIn particular, the equation above tells us that \n![](images/72/724f42afb534edbde7588c5508a4bf8de6d3cd068bc2989da7c7207b1bf7a1ba.svg)\nthat is to say, the numbers $\\color{red}-2,$ $\\color{red}0,$ $\\color{red}5$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nThe basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is somewhat special, since the coordinates of a vector relative to this basis coincide with the components of the vector. We refer to $\\mathcal{S}$ as the **standard basis**. Remember that in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3$ we even have special notations for the vectors of the standard basis:\n![](images/ae/aee5dd3226ebe950defc8b873399a884e0679ce701a3a55354ef01960e029f8e.svg)\n\n![](images/dd/dd96e0b70173adf8983feb4975c351ab3e417c34ed4a6a944bc391e33db4c8e9.png)\n\nIn general, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n![](images/a8/a8e4b648898c0dc036e228fb84945833dc40c053beff034596e098c4dd077e23.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121066 #edb/ref 1065 3969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121066 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "54e7c1fd-c7bf-5c77-bcc5-eb8d93a11582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121066 #edb/ref 1067 "The Standard Basis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409787 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0470cd3b-2bcb-575e-9def-d43004406207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409800 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409800 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409800 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a3e208c3-ba1a-51ec-b367-c2784fabe06c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409801 #edb/ref 1080 "x\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409801 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409801 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5ba42a7-b00b-5980-9f89-48160c1eb49e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409806 #edb/ref 1080 "-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409806 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409806 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d13c159c-f442-5933-a821-d94880c3ba32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409809 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409809 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409809 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eec553dd-6fd7-5208-9292-850213e9c19b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409812 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a79bee7d-12b6-5017-ae13-ca00a861bd24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409821 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409821 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec45397c-5ced-5a47-b9a9-86c6aa1d1bf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409821 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ \\,\\,-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\,\\,0=5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the third equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
